  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for heavy rain continues across the southeast today. \nThe Flood Watch remains in effect for northeast North Carolina \nthrough tonight.\n\nA cold front is situated south of the area, now draped across \ncentral Carolinas. A mid level trough is positioned over the east \ncoast with the axis into central VA. The 24 hr observed rainfall \namounts show a stark contrast between northern and southern portions \nof the area with areas north of Richmond generally seeing less than \n0.25\", while southern VA portions range from 0.50-3\" with the \nhighest totals in NE NC ranging from 1-4\". The cold front has moved \nfurther south than originally expected, which has allowed for \namounts to be slightly lower than anticipated from more scattered \nthan widespread showers and storms today. However, the latest radar \nshows a few showers continuing across the SE. The 12z models show a \nlull in showers this evening with more scattered showers early \nFriday morning possible across the SE. PWAT values between 2-2.2\" \nare still evident over the SE, which could allow for heavy rainfall \nwith any shower. Despite the saturated atmosphere, the Flood Watch \nhas been dropped for the southside Hampton Roads area as models show \nthe heavier bands of rain remaining south of the area, as well as \nlowered 24 hr totals than anticipated. The Flood Watch remains in \neffect for the northeast North Carolina counties through midnight \ntonight as an additional 2-3\" is possible. \n\nFor the rest of the area, dewpoints have fallen as low as the upper \n50s in Louisa County with temperatures in the mid 70s which has \nallowed for the day to feel quite comfortable and almost fall-like. \nTonight's temperatures will be cool with lows in the 60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures are expected tomorrow \nthrough the weekend, before trending back to seasonal for late July \nearly next week. There are daily chances for afternoon storms, but \noverall drier than the current wet pattern.\n\nThe cold front will remain south of the area through the weekend, \ndeveloping a weak low along the front over central North Carolina. \nResidual moisture may bring more showers to SE portions of the area \nas the low pushes additional chances into the area. The FA will then \nbe positioned under an UL trough over the weekend with onshore flow \nin place at the sfc. This will lead to cooler, below-average \ntemperatures for a few days. Highs on Fri and Sat will be in the \nupper 70s to around 80. Warming up slightly for Sunday with highs in \nthe low to mid 80s. Near-average temps return early next week. \nLooking to be overall a dry weekend, but a few isolated \nshowers/storms cannot be ruled out during the afternoons.",
